@@ -0,0 +1,71 @@
+## Deploying Fizzy on a single server with Kamal
+
+Fizzy includes a ready-to-use [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org/) configuration (`config/deploy.yml` and `.kamal/secrets`) for running everything in a single container on one Linux server with SQLite, local file storage, background jobs (Solid Queue), and automatic SSL via `kamal-proxy`.
+
+### Prerequisites
+
+- A Linux server with SSH access (Docker will be installed automatically by `kamal setup` if needed).
+- A domain name with an `A` (and/or `AAAA`) DNS record pointing to your server's IP address.
+- An SMTP mail server for sending magic-link sign-in codes and notification emails.
+
+### 1. Configure your server and hostname
+
+Edit `config/deploy.yml`:
+
+- Under `servers.web`, replace `your-server-ip-or-hostname` with your server's IP address or SSH hostname.
+- Under `proxy.host`, replace `your-domain.example.com` with your domain name (for example, `fizzy.example.com`).
+- Under `env.clear.APP_HOST`, set the same domain name so links in emails, push notifications, and webhooks use `https://your-domain.example.com`.
+
+### 2. Configure your mail server
+
+Under `env.clear` in `config/deploy.yml`, set your SMTP details:
+
+- `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS`: Sender address (for example, `Fizzy <noreply@your-domain.example.com>`).
+- `SMTP_ADDRESS`: Your SMTP server host.
+- `SMTP_PORT`: SMTP port (`587` for STARTTLS, `465` for implicit TLS).
+- `SMTP_USERNAME`: Your SMTP username (remove or leave blank if your mail relay does not require authentication).
+
+### 3. Configure secrets
+
+Generate your secrets locally:
+
+```sh
+# Generate SECRET_KEY_BASE
+bin/rails secret
+
+# Generate VAPID keys for web push notifications
+bin/rails runner 'k = WebPush.generate_key; puts "VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=#{k.public_key}\nVAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=#{k.private_key}"'
+```
+
+By default, `.kamal/secrets` reads `SECRET_KEY_BASE`, `SMTP_PASSWORD`, `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY`, and `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY` from your local environment (or you can configure a password manager adapter in `.kamal/secrets`).
+
+Export them before deploying (or load them from a gitignored `.env` file):
+
+```sh
+export SECRET_KEY_BASE="..."
+export SMTP_PASSWORD="..."
+export VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY="..."
+export VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY="..."
+```
+
+### 4. Deploy
+
+Commit your changes to `config/deploy.yml` (and `.kamal/secrets` if modified — never commit raw secret values), then run:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+Once `kamal setup` completes, Fizzy will be available at `https://your-domain.example.com`.
+
+For subsequent deployments, run:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+### How it works
+
+- **Single container**: Puma runs together with Thruster (port 80) and Solid Queue (`SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA=true`), so web requests, WebSockets (Solid Cable), caching (Solid Cache), and background/recurring jobs all run on the box in one container.
+- **Persistent data**: All SQLite databases (`storage/production*.sqlite3`) and Active Storage uploads live in `/rails/storage`, backed by the `fizzy_storage` Docker volume so data survives future deploys.
+- **SSL**: `kamal-proxy` automatically provisions and renews Let's Encrypt TLS certificates for your `proxy.host` and terminates HTTPS in front of the container.
